from 1 through 30 and 20 red balls numbered from 1 through 20.  In each lottery drawing, 3 of the white balls and 2 of the red balls are drawn.  To win, you must match all 3 white balls and both red balls, without regard to the order in which they were drawn.  How many possible different combinations may be drawn?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a0d41f489918841f3825ef8c5888ec8ff1afb6240508b9de80cbc5dc6c5b532d"},"seconds":12.545760869979858,"seed":1003854268628668764,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the number of possible different combinations that can be drawn in the lottery, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 3 white balls from 30 and 2 red balls from 20. The order in which the balls are drawn does not matter, so we use combinations (denoted as \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\)), which represent the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) items from \\( n \\) items without regard to order.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 white balls from 30. This is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{30}{3} = \\frac{30!}{3!(30-3)!} = \\frac{30!}{3! \\cdot 27!} = \\frac{30 \\times 29 \\times 28 \\times 27!}{3! \\times 27!} = \\frac{30 \\times 29 \\times 28}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{24360}{6} = 4060\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 red balls from 20:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20!}{2!(20-2)!} = \\frac{20!}{2! \\cdot 18!} = \\frac{20 \\times 19 \\times 18!}{2! \\times 18!} = \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{380}{2} = 190\n\\]\n\nTo find the total number of possible different combinations that can be drawn, we multiply the number of ways to choose the white balls by the number of ways to choose the red balls:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{30}{3} \\times \\binom{20}{2} = 4060 \\times 190 = 771400\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of possible different combinations that may be drawn is \\(\\boxed{771400}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{771400}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"counting_and_probability:90"}
